{
  "image_name": "5ae120f34b66a0d79cc243c12405936f.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes the main properties used to describe and distinguish carbon nanotube materials. Rather than showing one experiment, it organizes the key sources of variability that can affect how nanotubes behave.\n\nThe graphic highlights five broad categories. One is **surface defects**, including lattice defects, attached chemical groups, and substitutional doping, illustrated as disruptions in the nanotube’s hexagonal wall. A second is **physical structure**, emphasizing nanotube length, diameter, chirality, number of walls, and aspect ratio. Another category is **impurities**, showing both carbon-based byproducts and residual metallic catalyst particles or dopant species associated with production. The figure also points to **surface wrapping**, where surfactants, polymers, or biomolecules coat the nanotube exterior, changing its interface with the environment. Finally, it shows **aggregation state**, contrasting individually dispersed nanotubes with bundled or more entangled/aligned forms.\n\nTaken together, the figure conveys that a “carbon nanotube” is not a single uniform substance but a material class defined by multiple structural and chemical features. The note at the bottom with a CAS number suggests that one registry label can cover nanotubes that still differ substantially in composition and morphology.",
